 # Task recovery
 
-On a failed or interrupted task, identify the last verified state, preserve completed work, and resume only the outstanding authorized actions. Check the resulting behavior and distinguish an external blocker from completed delivery. Keep recovery notes within the active project scope.
+On a failed or interrupted task, identify the last verified state, preserve completed work, and resume only outstanding authorized actions. Save the artifact and next action before a fragile operation. Reconcile completed external actions before retrying; an interruption does not justify replaying them.
+
+Use finite operation deadlines when the host exposes them. If a tool fails or exceeds its declared bound, use an already available suitable fallback or checkpoint the blocker. Do not repeat an unchanged failed path without a new premise. If the host cannot enforce a deadline or cancel a blocked operation, state that limit; a timeout instruction is not runtime enforcement.
+
+Verify the recovered result. Distinguish elapsed time, recorded activity and observed failure from an unproven cause. Keep recovery notes within the active project scope and do not infer delivery, quality or autonomous continuation from configuration alone.
